     "text": "**Williams PLC Clinic**\n\n**Dear Colleague,**\n\nI saw Mr. Douglas Macdonald in consultation on February 7, 2025, for an annual physical. \n\n**History:**\n\nMr. Macdonald is a 45-year-old male with a significant past medical history of coronary artery disease (CAD), status post percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) with stent placement. His initial event occurred at age 43, approximately three years ago, where he underwent stent placement due to significant disease in two coronary vessels. At that time, his ejection fraction (EF) was documented at 59%, and his low-density lipoprotein (LDL) cholesterol level was 80 mg/dL. Currently, his condition is well controlled with medical therapy, which includes atorvastatin 80 mg daily, aspirin 81 mg daily, and metoprolol 25 mg daily. He has no history of angina and has not had any abnormal stress tests in the past 12 months.\n\nAdditionally, Mr. has a history of non-obstructive coronary artery disease, characterized by less than 1% stenosis, which is also currently well controlled without the need for additional medications.\n\nOn social history, Mr. Macdonald was a former smoker, having smoked one pack per day of cigarettes. He started smoking on June 15, 2019, and quit March 26, 2025. He reported his smoking cessation to be honest and truthful. Regarding alcohol consumption, he drinks approximately six drinks per week.\n\n**Exam:**\n\nOn examination today, Mr. Macdonald appeared to be in no acute His vital signs were within normal limits: blood pressure was 130/84 mmHg, and his weight was 171 pounds. The cardiovascular exam was notable for a regular rate and rhythm with no murmurs, rubs, or gallops. The respiratory exam revealed clear lung fields bilaterally with no wheezes, rales, or rhonchi. The abdominal exam was benign with no organomegaly or tenderness. Extremities were warm and well perfused with no edema.\n\n**Impression and Recommendations:**\n\nMr. Macdonald's coronary artery disease, both obstructive and non-obstructive, is currently well controlled with the outlined medical regimen. His lipid profile and cardiac status have been stable the past year with no episodes of angina or other ischemic symptoms. The recent stent placement three years ago has been effective in managing his disease, and there have been no complications or need for further revascularization.\n\nContinued management should focus on adherence to his current medications, lifestyle modifications, and regular follow-up. The patient has been compliant with his medication regimen and has made significant lifestyle changes, in"
